WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.842 --> 00:00:06.137
SBI, from the Center for Creative Leadership: situation, behaviour, impact.

2
00:00:07.175 --> 00:00:08.986
Situation: when and where.

3
00:00:09.681 --> 00:00:13.188
Behaviour: observable behaviour, what a camera would have recorded,

4
00:00:13.361 --> 00:00:14.731
without interpretation.

5
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.051
Impact: the effect on you, the team or the result.

6
00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:20.773
Then a question.

7
00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:26.180
In yesterday's workshop, you were on your phone for most of the second hour.

8
00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:30.958
Two operators went quiet; they thought it was already decided.

9
00:00:31.726 --> 00:00:32.748
What was going on?

10
00:00:34.018 --> 00:00:35.667
Behaviour, not character.

11
00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:38.900
You interrupted Ans twice: feedback.

12
00:00:39.642 --> 00:00:43.068
You're dominant: a label, and people defend against it.

13
00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:47.068
Tie the impact to their goal, team or project.

14
00:00:47.812 --> 00:00:52.027
One point per conversation; three at once becomes a performance review.

15
00:00:53.034 --> 00:00:56.239
Many trainers advise against the feedback sandwich:

16
00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:58.352
people brace and miss the message.

17
00:00:59.365 --> 00:01:02.895
Give it soon, within a day or two, in private and calm.

18
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.327
Not right before he presents.

19
00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:09.699
Positive feedback works the same way, and more often.

20
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.969
Specific praise builds credit; good job builds nothing.

21
00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.827
Upward feedback goes to someone above you in rank, like a senior client.

22
00:01:20.595 --> 00:01:22.952
Ask first: can I share an observation?

23
00:01:23.995 --> 00:01:27.037
About the shared goal, small, with: I noticed.

24
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:29.307
Then ask, and listen.

25
00:01:30.328 --> 00:01:34.251
If he pushes back, ask what he saw; don't argue the facts again.

26
00:01:34.951 --> 00:01:37.180
If he changes the subject, leave it.

27
00:01:38.252 --> 00:01:39.042
Receiving it?

28
00:01:39.805 --> 00:01:44.113
Thanks, ask for an example, don't defend, and come back later.

29
00:01:45.147 --> 00:01:47.446
Feedforward, Marshall Goldsmith's term,

30
00:01:47.643 --> 00:01:50.534
looks ahead: what should I do differently next session?

31
00:01:51.228 --> 00:01:53.945
Easier to answer honestly than: how did I do?

32
00:01:55.272 --> 00:01:59.358
At a food manufacturer, your sponsor, the operations director,

33
00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:02.144
answers every question to his team leads.

34
00:02:02.855 --> 00:02:05.084
After the fourth, they go quiet.

35
00:02:06.145 --> 00:02:08.246
You need them to own the actions.

36
00:02:08.956 --> 00:02:11.208
After lunch: can I share an observation?

37
00:02:11.917 --> 00:02:13.798
It's about what you asked me for.

38
00:02:14.865 --> 00:02:19.544
Then SBI: on the changeover data, you answered the first four questions.

39
00:02:20.338 --> 00:02:24.251
Then Peter and Sandra went quiet, and I need their commitment.

40
00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:27.827
Ask, and stop talking: how did you see it?

41
00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:31.263
He agrees: next time they answer first.

42
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.407
A performance conversation is short and regular, about the numbers,

43
00:02:36.604 --> 00:02:39.565
with whoever owns them: facts, cause, commitment.

44
00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:44.251
Most reviews jump from a red number straight to try harder.

45
00:02:45.235 --> 00:02:48.068
Facts first, over six to eight points.

46
00:02:48.784 --> 00:02:51.837
If the data is disputed, fix the data first.

47
00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.153
Crew lead Mark, at a grid operator.

48
00:02:55.854 --> 00:02:58.733
Target: 85 percent on the planned day.

49
00:02:59.404 --> 00:03:03.874
Four weeks: 84, 86, 83, and 70.

50
00:03:04.852 --> 00:03:06.187
Mark: it was the weather.

51
00:03:06.918 --> 00:03:09.786
But 28 of 40 is clearly below normal.

52
00:03:11.134 --> 00:03:14.919
Then the cause, with an open question: where did the jobs get stuck?

53
00:03:15.979 --> 00:03:21.052
Separate a one-off, like a storm, from a pattern, like materials late every Monday.

54
00:03:22.032 --> 00:03:24.726
5 from the storm, 7 from materials.

55
00:03:25.462 --> 00:03:28.097
Accept the storm; talk about the materials.

56
00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:31.874
The commitment: action, owner, date, check.

57
00:03:32.617 --> 00:03:35.717
Mark checks materials with the warehouse every Thursday.

58
00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:40.245
Hard on the numbers, soft on the people: learning, not blame.

59
00:03:41.246 --> 00:03:44.915
What goes wrong: a status round, actions without an owner.

60
00:03:45.666 --> 00:03:47.233
The owner speaks first.

61
00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.043
One person's performance is a private talk.

62
00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:54.420
Ask: what did we agree, and did it work?

63
00:03:55.771 --> 00:03:58.093
Disagreeing upwards is part of the job:

64
00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:00.635
clients don't hire you for their own opinion.

65
00:04:01.648 --> 00:04:04.214
Edmondson researched psychological safety:

66
00:04:04.353 --> 00:04:06.896
teams where it is safe to speak up learn more.

67
00:04:07.889 --> 00:04:12.173
Keep fact, view and request apart: what the data shows, what you think,

68
00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:13.752
and the step you ask for.

69
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:16.102
For a big disagreement,

70
00:04:16.242 --> 00:04:20.120
one-on-one first; never surprise someone in front of their team.

71
00:04:20.832 --> 00:04:23.607
In a meeting, a question, calm and low.

72
00:04:24.898 --> 00:04:27.394
Steering group at a parts manufacturer.

73
00:04:28.097 --> 00:04:32.671
The COO: operators don't follow the schedule; it becomes a bonus criterion.

74
00:04:33.688 --> 00:04:34.721
You are the youngest.

75
00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.280
Ask calmly: can I add one thing from the data?

76
00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:45.337
The fact: 84 of the 120 late orders, 70 percent, came from materials.

77
00:04:46.066 --> 00:04:48.330
Your view: a bonus may not move it.

78
00:04:49.375 --> 00:04:53.020
Your request: materials first, the bonus next month.

79
00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.385
If he holds his view, ask for fifteen minutes afterwards.

80
00:04:58.675 --> 00:05:03.087
Say it clearly once, and a second time only with new information.

81
00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:08.101
If it isn't about safety or integrity: disagree and commit.

82
00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.511
Carry it out fully, and note your advice.

83
00:05:12.518 --> 00:05:16.640
If it is, take the escalation path: openly to the next level up,

84
00:05:16.848 --> 00:05:18.264
telling the person first.

85
00:05:19.311 --> 00:05:23.804
Failure modes: hinting too softly, winning in public and losing the person,

86
00:05:23.955 --> 00:05:25.406
or going behind their back.

87
00:05:26.414 --> 00:05:29.549
Disagree and commit is often attributed to Intel,

88
00:05:29.735 --> 00:05:31.929
made known by Jeff Bezos of Amazon.

89
00:05:32.644 --> 00:05:35.755
It only works if the disagreement was truly heard.

90
00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:40.193
A sharp question: what would you need to see to change your mind?
